            "text": "Rounds every value of a tensor to the nearest whole number - or, with decimals, to the nearest tenth, hundredth, ten or hundred.\n\nUse it to snap continuous values onto a grid: a fader position onto whole steps, a pitch onto semitones, a coordinate onto a cell. Each element is rounded on its own, so the output has the shape of the input.\n\nHALVES GO TO THE EVEN NEIGHBOUR:\nA value exactly half-way, like 0.5 or 2.5, goes to whichever neighbour is EVEN: 0.5 becomes 0, 1.5 becomes 2, 2.5 becomes 2, -0.5 becomes -0. This is deliberate and standard (always rounding halves up adds a small bias that builds up over many values), but it surprises people who expect 0.5 to become 1. If you want halves always up, add 0.5 and use t.floor.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.round\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nt.round\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor. A list, a NumPy array or a single number is turned into a float32 tensor first. Every input is processed and sent - this is the only inlet that makes the node send. An empty tensor sends nothing.\n\ndecimals (int box, also an inlet):\nHow many decimal places to keep. Default 0, whole numbers. 1 rounds to tenths, 2 to hundredths. Negative values round to the left of the point: -1 to tens, -2 to hundreds (halves still go even: 15 and 25 both become 20). Changing it does not send by itself; the next tensor uses it.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe rounded tensor, the same shape and type as the input - still floats, just with whole-number values.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nThe ramp climbs straight from -2.5 to 2.5; t.round turns it into a staircase of flat steps at -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, each step centred on its whole number and rising half-way between. Set decimals to 1 and the steps become ten times finer, almost hugging the ramp. The message below (sent when the page opens, and again when clicked) shows the half-way values going to even numbers.\n\nRELATED:\nt.floor, t.ceil and t.trunc turn fractions into whole numbers in the other three ways - they differ in which way they go. t.frac keeps the part t.trunc throws away. round does the same for numbers and NumPy arrays (np.round shares its page)."
        },
